vl53l7cx_stop_ranging() must not report success for a stop it did not confirm.

TWO PLACES IN ONE FUNCTION LOSE A FAILURE THE SAME WAY, by folding a byte into the
status with |= when the byte that says "this went wrong" is zero.

THE POLL TIMEOUT. The function provokes an MCU stop and then polls G02 status 0 for
bit 7, for up to five seconds. On timeout it did `status |= tmp` and broke -- where
`tmp` is the last byte read, and the loop can only still be running because bit 7 was
clear. So `tmp` is in 0x00..0x7F, and a device that keeps answering 0x00 -- the
ordinary reading for "not stopped", and the reading a bus that is simply not
answering also produces through a zeroing port -- left `status` exactly as it was.
Five seconds of waiting then returned VL53L7CX_STATUS_OK. That timeout is now a
failure in its own right, with the status the ULD already defines for it.

THE STATUS 1 CHECK below it had the same shape. When the device did stop, the
function reads G02 status 1 and treats anything other than 0x84 or 0x85 as wrong --
then recorded it with `status |= tmp`. A status 1 of 0x00 is not 0x84 and not 0x85,
so it took the failure branch, contributed nothing, and the function returned OK for
a stop whose own reported state the ULD had just rejected. An unaccepted status is
now reported explicitly.

IT IS NOT LABELLED A TIMEOUT, because it is not one: the device answered, promptly,
with a state this ULD does not accept. VL53L7CX_STATUS_ERROR is the ULD's own generic
failure and is what an unaccepted status becomes.

WHY THE DEVICE BYTE IS DISCARDED RATHER THAN CARRIED, which is a reversal of this
patch's first version and is the point of its second. That version kept `status |= tmp`
and added the substitute only when the OR had produced nothing, so that "wherever
`tmp` does carry a device code, that code is still what comes back". The promise cannot
be kept: the caller classifies this return value by EXACT MATCH, and the ULD's own
codes live in the same 8 bits as the device byte. TIMEOUT_ERROR is 0x01,
CORRUPTED_FRAME 0x02, MCU_ERROR 0x42, INVALID_PARAM 0x7F. So a timeout whose last
`tmp` was 0x02 came back as 0x03 and was classified as a generic I/O error rather than
a timeout; a status 1 of 0x01 came back as 0x01 and was classified as a TIMEOUT by a
branch whose own comment says it must not be -- the device had answered promptly. One
8-bit value cannot be both a classification and a raw device byte, and of the two it is
the classification the caller needs: tof_l7_sensor::mapped_error() turns it into the
errno, and the boot-time recovery pass tells its cases apart by that errno.

The device byte is therefore dropped in these two branches, and nothing else about
this function's accounting changes. A PRE-EXISTING NON-ZERO STATUS IS PRESERVED: the
substitution happens only when `status` is still OK, so a transport failure recorded
by one of the RdByte/WrByte calls above is never overwritten by either of these. Those
failures also reach the caller by a second route that does not pass through this value
at all -- tof_l7_sensor::finish() returns the port's own errno when the port recorded
one, and consults the ULD status only when it did not.

WHY THIS MATTERS HERE MORE THAN IT DOES UPSTREAM. The boot-time recovery pass exists
to put a surviving sensor into a known state over I2C, and it decides what to report
from this return value. "Stopped" when nothing was confirmed is the one answer it
must not produce: it turns an unrecovered survivor into a clean boot record, and the
next failure is attributed somewhere else.

Applied to the copy in the build directory, never in place, so SHA256SUMS continues
to answer whether upstream changed.
diff --git a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
--- a/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
+++ b/modules/vl53l7cx_api.c
@@ -651,7 +651,10 @@
 
 	        	if(timeout > (uint16_t)500)
 				{
-					status |= tmp;
+					if(status == VL53L7CX_STATUS_OK)
+					{
+						status = VL53L7CX_STATUS_TIMEOUT_ERROR;
+					}
 					break;
 				}
         	}
@@ -662,7 +665,9 @@
 	if((tmp & (uint8_t)0x80) != (uint8_t)0){
 		status |= VL53L7CX_RdByte(&(p_dev->platform), 0x7, &tmp);
 		if((tmp != (uint8_t)0x84) && (tmp != (uint8_t)0x85)){
-		   status |= tmp;
+		   if(status == VL53L7CX_STATUS_OK){
+		      status = VL53L7CX_STATUS_ERROR;
+		   }
 		}
 	}
 
